1. Create a basic sentence structure with placeholders for the random words.
2. Use an array or list of words to store possible options to replace placeholders.
3. Use the `Random` class to pick words randomly from the lists and construct the sentence.

Here's an example of how to do this in C#:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class RandomSentenceGenerator
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        Random rnd = new Random();

        // Define lists of randomly picked words
        List<string> adjectives = new List<string>() { "colorful", "amazing", "sleepy", "angry", "happy" };
        List<string> verbs = new List<string>() { "plays with", "jumps over", "chases", "uses", "watches" };
        List<string> nouns = new List<string>() { "mouse", "keyboard", "table", "chair", "mousepad" };

        // Basic sentence structure for using "cat" and "computer"
        string sentenceTemplate = "The {0} cat {1} the {2} computer with the {3} {4}.";

        // Select random words from each list
        string randomAdjective1 = adjectives[rnd.Next(adjectives.Count)];
        string randomAdjective2 = adjectives[rnd.Next(adjectives.Count)];
        string randomVerb = verbs[rnd.Next(verbs.Count)];
        string randomNoun = nouns[rnd.Next(nouns.Count)];
        string randomAdjective3 = adjectives[rnd.Next(adjectives.Count)];

        // Use placeholders to insert random words into sentence
        string resultSentence = string.Format(sentenceTemplate, randomAdjective1, randomVerb, randomAdjective2, randomAdjective3, randomNoun);

        // Print the randomly generated sentence
        Console.WriteLine(resultSentence);
    }
}
```
This code will generate sentences like:
```
The amazing cat chases the sleepy computer with the colorful keyboard.
The happy cat jumps over the colorful computer with the angry mousepad.
