I appreciate the challenge! Here's a creative iterative approach to compute the factorial of a number without using recursion in Java:
```java
public class Factorial {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 5;
        long fact = factorialize(n);
        System.out.println("Factorial of " + n + " is: " + fact);
    }

    public static long factorialize(int n) {
        long fact = 1;
        int count = 1;

        while (count <= n) {
            fact *= count++;
        }

        return fact;
    }
}
```
And here's a strategy to mitigate stack overflow issues when using recursion for computing the factorial. We can use a tail call optimization:
```java
public class Factorial {
    private static int limit = 1000; // Adjust this value to avoid stack overflow
    private static long[] memoization = new long[limit];

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 15;
        long fact = factorialize(n);
        System.out.println("Factorial of " + n + " is: " + fact);
    }

    public static long factorialize(int n, int memoIndex) {
        if (n <= 1) {
            return 1;
        }

        if (memoIndex == limit) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Value of n is too large for the given limit");
        }

        if (memoization[memoIndex] != 0) {
            return memoization[memoIndex];
        }

        long result = n * factorialize(n - 1, memoIndex + 1);
        memoization[memoIndex] = result;
        return result;
    }
}
```
In this implementation, we maintain a memoization array with a limited size. If the array is full, and a new value is encountered, we throw an exception to indicate that the value of n is too large for the given limit. This approach effectively prevents stack overflow errors while still using the recursive function for computing the factorial.

